{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '1,190p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Middleware\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which wins on any disagreement. Every\n     5\tsentence below is a rule. This document names no network; the network\n     6\tattachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n     7\tsection 7.\n     8\t\n     9\t## What a middleware is\n    10\t\n    11\tA middleware is a value: a map of two transforms, one over the operation\n    12\tmap on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. Each\n    13\ttransform is a total function from its input map to its output map, with\n    14\tthree bounded exceptions named below: the gate's read on its composed\n    15\tdecision medium (a handle-local read cursor and cached decision, advanced\n    16\tonly by the gate itself), a metering emission to a composed side stream,\n    17\tand the gate's one recovery re-read after a gap. These are stream\n    18\temissions and handle-local reads, never a retry of the wrapped operation\n    19\tand never a wait: the wrapped operation is issued exactly once. It\n    20\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    21\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    22\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    23\t\n    24\t```clojure\n    25\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    26\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    27\t```\n    28\t\n    29\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    30\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    31\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    32\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    33\t\n    34\t## The operation map\n    35\t\n    36\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    37\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    38\t\n    39\t```clojure\n    40\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next |\n    41\t                          :dao.stream/append!\n    42\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    43\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    47\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    48\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    49\t\n    50\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    51\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    52\t\n    53\t## wrap\n    54\t\n    55\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    56\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    57\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    58\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    59\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    60\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    61\t\n    62\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    63\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    64\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    65\t\n    66\t## The position rule\n    67\t\n    68\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    69\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    70\t\n    71\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    72\t  cursor;\n    73\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    74\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    75\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    76\t\n    77\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    78\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    79\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    80\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    81\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    82\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    83\tmiddleware.\n    84\t\n    85\t## The four prohibitions\n    86\t\n    87\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over any handle,\n    88\tnever retries the wrapped operation, never waits, and never touches the\n    89\tinner handle: the only handle operations it may perform are the decision\n    90\tread `gate` defines below (a bounded read on a composed decision medium,\n    91\twhose gap recovery re-reads that medium and whose cursor re-minting after\n    92\ta non-`ok` outcome mints afresh next operation; neither retries the\n    93\twrapped operation) and one `append!` to a\n    94\tside stream it was composed with. It reads no host clock. A transform that\n    95\tneeds facts from a stream is not a middleware: folding a stream is an\n    96\tinterpreter's work, and what a middleware reads is a decision an\n    97\tinterpreter already published onto a medium.\n    98\t\n    99\t## Failure inside a transform\n   100\t\n   101\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n   102\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n   103\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n   104\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n   105\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n   106\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n   107\t\n   108\t## Two generic middlewares\n   109\t\n   110\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n   111\tspecified here.\n   112\t\n   113\t```clojure\n   114\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify   verify\n   115\t       :dao.stream.middleware/decision h})\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   119\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   120\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   121\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling. A decision is\n   122\twhatever value the index publishes; the gate reads none of it. It may be an\n   123\timmutable query value, a relation value or an opened published index, in\n   124\twhich case `verify` is a query over it with the request as bindings: the\n   125\tshape `dao.space.query/q` has (a query over immutable inputs, opening\n   126\tnothing), and the required adapter contract for a query interpreter that\n   127\tplugs into the gate (`dao.shibi.md`, How it plugs in).\n   128\t\n   129\t`wrap` calls `cursor` with `:dao.stream/oldest` once when it constructs\n   130\tthe gate. The medium must be open and readable; it has capacity one and\n   131\tevicts its older value. If cursor minting is not `ok`, the gate has no read\n   132\tcursor or decision. Each operation retries `cursor` once; if it is still\n   133\tnot `ok`, `next` is skipped and `verify` receives the none marker. A\n   134\tsuccessful mint is its read cursor.\n   135\t\n   136\tOn each operation, the gate calls `next` once at its read cursor. On `ok`,\n   137\tit stores the successor cursor and value. On `blocked`, it keeps the cursor\n   138\tand last value. On `end`, it clears the value and becomes ended. On `gap`,\n   139\tit clears the value, adopts the recovery cursor, and calls `next` once more.\n   140\tIf that call is `ok`, it stores the successor and value; if `blocked`, it\n   141\tkeeps the recovery cursor without a value; if `end`, it becomes ended; if\n   142\t`gap`, it adopts that recovery cursor and remains without a value. It does\n   143\tnot read a third time in this operation. On any other non-`ok` outcome,\n   144\tit clears the value and cursor, then retries cursor minting next operation.\n   145\t\n   146\t`verify` receives the last value observed, or\n   147\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}` when there is none, or\n   148\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/ended true}` once the gate is ended (the medium\n   149\tclosed; no later operation reads). That value is a bounded snapshot, not a\n   150\tpromise that it is the newest concurrent write.\n   151\t`nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits with\n   152\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   153\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   154\trefuses the marker. The cursor and value are state of the\n   155\twrapped handle, supplied to `wrap`, not global state. The composition must\n   156\tserialize calls on one wrapped handle; the gate has no queue, lock or wait.\n   157\tThe decision medium is not the wrapped stream, so the position rule on the\n   158\tinner handle is untouched.\n   159\t\n   160\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   161\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   162\tit was composed with: this is **decision emission**, and an index that\n   163\tobserves that stream is how metered use keeps its state on a stream\n   164\trather than in the gate.\n   165\t\n   166\t**present**, the credential step.\n   167\t\n   168\t```clojure\n   169\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   170\t```\n   171\t\n   172\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   173\t`out` is identity.\n   174\t\n   175\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   176\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   177\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   178\t[`dao.shibi.md`](./dao.shibi.md).\n   179\t\n   180\t## Exemplars\n   181\t\n   182\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   183\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   184\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   185\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   186\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   187\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   188\t\n   189\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   190\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'tail -80 collab/1790458820302-architect-dao-stream-remote-final-gate-r2.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a0dfa9-78af-7663-a4d2-43f70690ec64\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll check the four changes in the working tree, then review the affected spec sections for issues introduced by the wraps. I’ll leave the files untouched.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\\n\\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\\n\\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\\n\\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\\nnot an upward callback.\\n\\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\\nfor facts it owns.\\n\\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\\n\\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\\n\\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: code-review-pr\\ndescription: >-\\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\\n---\\n\\n# Code Review PR Skill\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\\n\\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\\n\\n# 2. Review last commit\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\\n\\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\\n\\n# 4. Save review report to file\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Code Review Workflow\\n\\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\\n\\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\\n\\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\\n\\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\\n\\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\\n\\n```markdown\\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\\n\\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\\n  ```diff\\n  - problematic code\\n  + suggested fix\\n  ```\\n\\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\\n\\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\\n\\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Utility Script Reference\\n\\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\\n\\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Common Review Pitfalls\\n\\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git status --short && git diff --stat && rg --files | rg '(dao\\\\\\\\.stream\\\\\\\\.remote|middleware|implementation-plan)\\\\\\\\.md\\\"'$'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git status --short && git diff --stat && rg --files | rg '(dao\\\\\\\\.stream\\\\\\\\.remote|middleware|implementation-plan)\\\\\\\\.md\\\"'$'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2026-09-27 04:40:35.052 xcodebuild[31603:27236272]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-09-27 04:40:35.360 xcodebuild[31603:27236271] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-RMiEkUw7' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-09-27 04:40:35.971 xcodebuild[31622:27236320]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-09-27 04:40:36.158 xcodebuild[31622:27236319] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n M docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md\\n M docs/design/dao.data.btree.md\\n M docs/design/dao.jing.call-site-classification.md\\n M docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md\\n M docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\\n M docs/design/dao.